#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/id #uuid "b7cba6bc-39b5-5e03-ba33-c1fc2a1b4ae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21735" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4x(0) & =0 \\\\ 5x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)(1-0.2(2))-(4)(2)y & =0 \\\\ 10(1-0.4)-8y & =0 \\\\ 10(0.6)-8y & =0 \\\\ 6-8y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2, 0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n(5, 0), \\qquad\n(2, 0.75).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4936474f-5e5f-5da2-8242-1b48336a836c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Stable Case"}] :question/id #uuid "3e280ec5-c95a-5ca6-ba2e-edc4cf653ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21736" :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=5x(1-x)-xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-x)-xy)=5-10x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/b0/b046e51c9a1b68cc22e1bd46f532a8d8d70452e5549937aaa8d872e60c63eb20.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/e9/e91d26ba55bafc85ce58c65482858d3f2094136b9620fe5d6a30b514ffe59b87.png) to get\n\n![](images/dc/dcdb4709be5d408bf054292b36e9bd898db3047b4c66230fc9249fc57e229b2b.png)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-5(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2.5+\\lambda )+2.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2.5\\lambda +2.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = -1.25 \\pm 0.25\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable spiral sink."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Predator Extinction Case"}] :question/id #uuid "3076e962-385d-5c19-8179-7c1a91aa6e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-21738" :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-3xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=4-16x-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/4d/4d0b2b480e0423052a4461a0cae9c8b1fa7bb61d3a1ca4d20ed84e4e5e7bc874.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/08/08337f194eb5fb80b86f88f62978e79bb1d1b4e87c0862458e1603b930cba6af.png) to get\n\n![](images/15/1548452943d27f02cd2651cc8affb4800369e22e4685fc818fdd390d359bde18.png)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -4$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable sink."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/id #uuid "c8159388-bbcc-5204-8ed3-e9c2b9157d59" :question/math-academy-id "e-21739" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x) - 3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $6x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y = 0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71bba99b-2878-5cf7-939d-3472d3c673da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71bba99b-2878-5cf7-939d-3472d3c673da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370385} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafac934-f53f-573e-9679-5dd5e6863862" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7833a450-0184-58b0-b600-65ac5258889b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335566" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.2x(1-0.05x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-1.6xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.2x(1-0.05x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=20.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef00333a-9645-565b-82d3-8f42ebfe86b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef00333a-9645-565b-82d3-8f42ebfe86b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370391} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d164ef-2161-59b8-a136-7f7f4a1865ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e585eab-4fdd-53d8-83e1-a034834d3c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335574" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $2x(1-0.2x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=5.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "18a49556-8fde-5c11-8125-ba135426b69a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164714} {:answer-field/id #uuid "353326f8-9acc-57c1-af8e-a5a60d3c82e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164715} {:answer-field/id #uuid "48a65ede-be2c-5b54-bdde-4d97adb3efd9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164716}] :question/id #uuid "fc2a604f-ae58-5276-9fe8-1d80b5831936" :question/math-academy-id "q-335579" :question/problem "${x^{′} = 2x(1 - 0.2x) - xy; y^{′} =-4y + 2xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:$(0, 0), ($ {{field-1}} $, 0), ($ {{field-2}} $$, {{field-3}} $)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95c6bb5-0bea-53d7-8912-03fb824348e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d95c6bb5-0bea-53d7-8912-03fb824348e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370397} :answer-field/id #uuid "926c62d3-33b1-5a68-9893-0017786e7108" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f999bb63-f180-5d3e-bfde-e9b5319a01a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f999bb63-f180-5d3e-bfde-e9b5319a01a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370399} :answer-field/id #uuid "919a3769-36fa-5a1a-8af2-ab2341d81379" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eef3c976-4bf6-5772-a2a0-3b1d04bedb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef3c976-4bf6-5772-a2a0-3b1d04bedb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370401} :answer-field/id #uuid "038665d6-3284-5218-82a4-c5a18c2023dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e7ec761-7feb-53eb-a7d4-70a4387f7a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-335586" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.5x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)(1-0.5(1))-(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1-y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9d18baa-dd05-52e3-948e-e347be54cd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9d18baa-dd05-52e3-948e-e347be54cd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370404} :answer-field/id #uuid "12432354-1310-5714-b859-2033e8bc3468" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b55f96e2-387f-5cd4-848a-38f6455daa58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55f96e2-387f-5cd4-848a-38f6455daa58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370406} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b4c9e82-43a5-5c43-855d-1579b97f2f26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9fa4a23-ae8c-552e-a785-f5acbecfd29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9fa4a23-ae8c-552e-a785-f5acbecfd29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370408} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc54130-e37c-530b-a892-8fda673a7f6a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e1ede85-c858-51fe-8bcb-872a442fe5f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335593" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.25x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.25x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.25x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.25(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(2,1.5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4936474f-5e5f-5da2-8242-1b48336a836c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Stable Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90ed3da8-afba-51f3-9c1e-2b468c045db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164718} {:answer/id #uuid "94acc274-4b87-5077-80fd-99709a6579d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164719} {:answer/id #uuid "97e0115c-49a5-51da-8adc-c075eab19a30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164720} {:answer/id #uuid "cfeafe00-79e7-51ad-b562-3cb7dab10611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164721} {:answer/id #uu